NUM-60: correct a bottom orifice below tailwater for submergence once

When the tailwater stands above a bottom orifice's crest, orifice_getInflow()
already takes the head difference h1 - h2 as the orifice's head, both to
decide whether it runs in its weir regime and in the weir formula. It then
also applied Villemonte's submergence factor, which assumes the head was
measured from the crest. The flow was too small in the weir regime and
jumped by the inverse of the factor where the weir and orifice formulas
meet (H*), so an inflow inside the jump had no steady state.
Apply the factor to side orifices only. Side orifices, bottom orifices with
the tailwater below the crest, and every orifice running full are unchanged.

Apply from the root of a Stormwater-Management-Model checkout at cb3e192b:
    git apply NUM-60_swmm530.patch

diff --git a/src/legacy/engine/link.c b/src/legacy/engine/link.c
index 31925e1..dd35be6 100644
--- a/src/legacy/engine/link.c
+++ b/src/legacy/engine/link.c
@@ -1928,7 +1928,9 @@ double orifice_getInflow(int j)
     q = dir * orifice_getFlow(j, k, head, f, Link[j].hasFlapGate);
 
     // --- apply Villemonte eqn. to correct for submergence
-    if ( f < 1.0 && h2 > hcrest )
+    //     (a bottom orifice below tailwater already uses the head
+    //     difference h1 - h2, so it is not corrected a second time)
+    if ( f < 1.0 && h2 > hcrest && Orifice[k].type == SIDE_ORIFICE )
     {
         ratio = (h2 - hcrest) / (h1 - hcrest);
         q *= pow( (1.0 - pow(ratio, 1.5)), 0.385);
